
       Pascal-OS/8 Brief Sheet              Version 1-0-E    SSRFC  U of Minn
                                            1982-04-28       JTE    Edit 04

       Compiling a Pascal program.       ___________________________
       If your Pascal program is on a file called "PROG.PS" then the command:
               .R P,SYS:PASCAL,PROG
       will run (execute) the Pascal compiler with your program as it's
       input. If there are no errors, the compiler will generate a "code
       file" called "PROG.PB" which you can execute.

       The compiler will also provide a listing if you specify a device
       and/or file after the program. For example:
               .R P,SYS:PASCAL,PROG,LP:
       will compile PROG.PS while listing it on the line printer. (LP: is an
       internal line printer handler, see below.)

       To get a cross-referenced listing of your program, do:
               .R P,SYS:IDMAP,PROG,LP:
       which produces a cross-referenced listing on the line printer (LP:).


       Executing a Pascal program       __________________________
       A "code file" called "PROG.PB" is executed by the command:
               .R P,PROG
       Device and/or file names can be "substituted" for program parameters
       by listing them after PROG :
               .R P,PROG,param1,param2,...

       To repeat the last PMD (Post Mortem Display) in the event you need a          ______          ___
       printed copy, do:
               .R P,SYS:PMD,program,SYS:PASCAL.PM,LP:    / will be revised


       Internal Device Handlers (character oriented input/output). Not all       ________________________
       are necessarily available (installation defined).

       TT:     OS/8 console handler, input line buffered and edited.
       TT0:    OS/8 console handler, immediate (no buffering or editing).
       LP:     line printer handler, input line buffered and edited.
       LP0:    line printer handler, immediate.
       IO:     message I/O; equal to TT: unless BATCH is running on the
               line printer, then IO: is equal to LP:.
       NULL:   null device (input: immediate eof, output: infinite sink).
       PT:     paper tape (high speed, input: reader, output: punch).
       RIO:    remote terminal device, input line buffered and edited.
       RIO0:   remote terminal device, immediate (don't like this name@!).
       CS:     control statement image (read-only).
       TEK:    TEK 4015-1 scope, input line buffered and edited.
       TEK0:   TEK 4015-1 scope, immediate.
       GIN:    TEK 4015-1 scope, no echo (input for GIN mode only).
       DIG:    SSRFC digitizer input kludge; also DK:, DN:, DT:.!


       New Default File Name Extensions for Pascal-OS/8:       ________________________________

       .PS     PascalSource - source files suitable for compiler input.
       .PB     PascalBinary - executable files produced by compiler.
       .PM     PostMortem - execution traceback dump file, produced by P8RTS.

       Also, these two standard OS/8 extensions are used:

       .LS     LiSting - compiler listing (also IDMAP, PFMAP, other outputs).
       .TM     TeMporaries - internal files, scratch files.




       Compiler Options control compiler behavior. Default settings are shown       ________ _______
       in parenthesis. These switch options appear in program comments as:
       {$L+,T-} or on the compiler call statement, as:
               .R P,SYS:PASCAL,program/L+,T-

       I+,I-   enable/disable interactive I/O for all text files         (I+)
       N+,N-   enable/disable line number reporting within PMD           (N+)
       L+,L-   enable/disable compilation listing
                                        (L- unless listing device/file given)
       P+,P-   enable/disable Post-Mortem-Dump information generation    (P+)
       S+,S-   enable/disable segmentation of subsequent procedure or
               function blocks                                           (S-)
       T+,T-   enable/disable compiler-generated run-time tests          (T+)
       V+,V-   enable/disable variable reporting within PMD.             (V+)
       X+,X-   allow/disallow use of extensions                          (X-)
       O+,O-   enable/disable options. If disabled, cannot be enabled    (O+)

       The following numeric options can only appear in program comments:

       Bn      set buffer factor to n, where 1<= n <= 512 (number of blocks).
               (default  is B1)
       Wn      set workspace to n, where n is in words. (Default is all
               memory.) No effect in this release (V 1-0-E).


       Terminal Controls (TT:, RIO:, LP:)  ('*' means TT: handler only)       _________________
       Name      Display Effect       ________________________
       Control-C "^C"    abort program, kill BATCH, return to OS/8.
       Control-B "^B" *  abort program, close files, traceback, abort BATCH.
       Control-A "^A" *  abort program, close files, abort BATCH.
       Control-D      *  debug enable: interpreter state frames.
       Control-E      *  debug disable: interp. and segment state frames.
       Control-F      *  debug enable: segment loader state frames.

       Control-S         X-OFF, hold output until X-ON.
       Control-Q         X-ON, resume output after X-OFF.
       Control-O         slough off (mute) output, any other key will resume.

       The following are effective during line-buffered input (TT:, RIO:, LP:).

       Control-H         Backspace, delete one character, erase from screen.
       Rubout or Delete  delete one character; if OS/8 scope bit not set, echos
                         as "\x\", the TTY convention; if OS/8 scope bit is set,
                         same behavior as BackSpace: erases from screen.
       Control-U "^U"    delete current input line.
       Line Feed         re-print current input line.
       Tab               perform OS/8 tabulation; always expanded to blanks.
       Carriage Return   terminate input line; if a command statement in
                         response to "%", recalls P8RTS after program execution.
       Escape    "^["    if command statement ("%" prompt), terminate input and
                         return to OS/8 at end of program execution (if any);
                         otherwise no meaning as program input.
       Control-Z "^Z"    if program input, cause EOF to become true (after end
                         of current input line, if any); no meaning if given in
                         a command-statement (may change).
